Who can… restyled — 18 Sept. Ali: "agree proposed who can buukd please".

The list answers a question asked in a hurry, and the two facts that matter — which pod, and
whether they are still here later — were the hardest things to pick out of it. The pod was the
faintest text on the row.

  1 the pod is a chip in that pod's own colour, so it is found by colour rather than read;
  2 days and nights separated — "who can do an airway now" and "at 4am" are different questions
    and the old list mixed them with no marker;
  3 sorted A to E then nights, instead of whatever order the staff array happened to be in;
  4 each row carries grade, shift and whether they hold the phone — somebody on 8–4 is no use at
    six o'clock and that was invisible;
  5 the chips carry counts, so "Airway 6" answers before a name is read, and a count of zero
    greys the chip rather than handing over an empty list;
  6 a line saying which day it is — the old dialog never said, so a screenshot meant nothing the
    next morning.

AND THE EMPTY STATE STOPS BEING AN EMPTY BOX. "Nobody on today is signed off for this" plus who
is on TOMORROW, because the person asking still has a job to do and the board already holds it.

Tests: render 318 passed / 1 failed (pre-existing, unrelated). Seven new assertions, driven
rather than read — the dialog is opened and its chips, counts, groups and pod chips checked,
because the whole change is what it looks like.
